Lab Environment Setup
Task 1: Verify Service Mesh Installation and Deploy Sample Application
Subtask 1.1: Verify Istio Installation
First, let's verify that the OpenShift Service Mesh is properly installed and running.

# Check if Istio system namespace exists
oc get namespace istio-system

# Verify Istio components are running
oc get pods -n istio-system

# Check Service Mesh Control Plane status
oc get smcp -n istio-system
Subtask 1.2: Create Project and Enable Service Mesh
Create a new project for our sample application and enable service mesh injection.

# Create a new project
oc new-project bookinfo-observability

# Create ServiceMeshMemberRoll to include our project
cat <<EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: maistra.io/v1
kind: ServiceMeshMemberRoll
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  members:
  - bookinfo-observability
EOF

# Verify the project is added to the mesh
oc get smmr -n istio-system -o yaml
Subtask 1.3: Deploy Bookinfo Sample Application
Deploy the Bookinfo application which will generate metrics for our observability setup.

# Deploy the Bookinfo application
oc apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.19/samples/bookinfo/platform/kube/bookinfo.yaml -n bookinfo-observability

# Create the Gateway for external access
cat <<EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-gateway
  namespace: bookinfo-observability
spec:
  selector:
    istio: ingressgateway
  servers:
  - port:
      number: 80
      name: http
      protocol: HTTP
    hosts:
    - "*"
EOF

# Create VirtualService for routing
cat <<EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: bookinfo
  namespace: bookinfo-observability
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - bookinfo-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        exact: /productpage
    - uri:
        prefix: /static
    - uri:
        exact: /login
    - uri:
        exact: /logout
    - uri:
        prefix: /api/v1/products
    route:
    - destination:
        host: productpage
        port:
          number: 9080
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Verify Application Deployment
# Check if all pods are running
oc get pods -n bookinfo-observability

# Wait for all pods to be ready
oc wait --for=condition=ready pod --all -n bookinfo-observability --timeout=300s

# Get the ingress gateway URL
export GATEWAY_URL=$(oc get route istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Gateway URL: http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage"
Task 2: Access Prometheus and Grafana for Metrics
Subtask 2.1: Access Prometheus Dashboard
Prometheus is the metrics collection system in the Istio service mesh. Let's access and explore it.

# Check if Prometheus is running
oc get pods -n istio-system | grep prometheus

# Create a route to access Prometheus (if not already exists)
oc expose service prometheus -n istio-system --name=prometheus-route

# Get Prometheus URL
export PROMETHEUS_URL=$(oc get route prometheus-route -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Prometheus URL: http://$PROMETHEUS_URL"
Subtask 2.2: Explore Prometheus Metrics
Open the Prometheus URL in your browser and explore the available metrics:

Navigate to the Prometheus web interface
Click on Status → Targets to see all monitored endpoints
Go to Graph tab and try these sample queries:
# Total requests to productpage service
istio_requests_total{destination_service_name="productpage"}

# Request rate for all services
rate(istio_requests_total[5m])

# Response time percentiles
histogram_quantile(0.99, rate(istio_request_duration_milliseconds_bucket[5m]))

# Error rate
rate(istio_requests_total{response_code!~"2.."}[5m])
Subtask 2.3: Access Grafana Dashboard
Grafana provides visualization capabilities for the metrics collected by Prometheus.

# Check if Grafana is running
oc get pods -n istio-system | grep grafana

# Create a route to access Grafana
oc expose service grafana -n istio-system --name=grafana-route

# Get Grafana URL
export GRAFANA_URL=$(oc get route grafana-route -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Grafana URL: http://$GRAFANA_URL"
Subtask 2.4: Generate Traffic for Metrics
Before exploring dashboards, let's generate some traffic to create meaningful metrics:

# Generate continuous traffic to the application
for i in {1..100}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i completed"
  sleep 2
done &

# You can also generate traffic with different patterns
# Generate some error traffic
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage/nonexistent" > /dev/null
  sleep 1
done &
